  "brief": "You are editing a sparse autoencoder (SAE) by hand. Setting: a Solver model answers a question, a Critic disagrees, and the Solver writes a second answer that either adopts the Critic's answer or keeps its own. The test your edit will face: at layer 25, once, at the final prompt token before the second answer, we replace the Solver's activation x with x' = x + 0.2\u00b7|x|\u00b7u. Here u is built from two feature sets of this SAE. The adoption set A and the retention set R each have one direction: the normalized sum of their features' unit decoder rows (u_A, u_R). In the \"both\" arm, an episode the Solver originally adopted gets u = normalize(u_R \u2212 u_A), and one it originally retained gets u = normalize(u_A \u2212 u_R). We count how often the second answer flips to the opposite behavior, compared with steering along random features of similar firing frequency.\n\nYour goal: edit the decoder rows of the 10 adoption features and the 10 retention features in `selected`, so that this steering flips more answers in both directions.\n\nRules:\n- Change only those 20 rows, and only through `apply_edit(...)`.\n- Keep each row's length.\n- Keep cosine \u2265 0.95 with its original row; `apply_edit` reports each row's cosine, and how the set directions changed.\n- Measure anything you like on the train data.\n- Do not fit models or optimize: no gradients, no regression, no automated search over values. Every number in an edit must be one you choose and can explain.\n\nWhat you have:\n- `X_train`, `y_train` (1 = adoption, 0 = retention), `meta_train`, and the SAE.\n- `Z_train`, the train codes. They're fixed, since the encoder never changes.\n- `encode`, `decode`, `separation(i)` and `top_examples(i, k)`.\n- `hints`: auto-generated descriptions of the 20 features, from train examples and the decoder's logit lens. They may be wrong; check them. Many contrast \"fictional vs. real\" entities, which largely tracks the dataset source (synthetic vs. MuSiQue), not behavior.\n\n`steer_test()` runs the real test on 200 fixed train episodes. It returns flips and the mean change in the candidate margin, log p(the other answer's first token) \u2212 log p(the original answer's first token), on the episodes whose two answers start with different tokens, for your current edit vs. the unedited SAE. Flips are noisy at this size; weigh the margin too.\n\nBudget: 40 code snippets, 4 `steer_test` calls and a token cap; you'll be told what's left after each turn. Each turn, reply with exactly one of: one fenced `python` block, `steer_test()`, or `DONE`. You will never see validation or test results.\n\nExplain your reasoning before each edit. Finish with DONE and a summary: what you changed, why, and what you'd expect.",
