  "claude_summary": "DONE\n\n## What I did\n\n**The lever.** The test's perturbation is `x' = x + 0.2\u00b7|x|\u00b7u` with `u` unit-norm, so *only the direction* of `normalize(u_A \u2212 u_R)` matters. My whole edit is aimed at pointing that one direction at a behavior axis measured in the train activations.\n\n**Finding the axis (measurements only, no fitting).**\n- The raw class-mean difference `mean(X|adopt) \u2212 mean(X|retain)` is 0.67-aligned with the mean-activation direction. A push along that axis mostly rescales the residual stream and is largely undone by downstream LayerNorm, so I projected the mean-activation axis out. The remainder separated classes slightly *better* (0.35/\u22120.41 sd in musique).\n- Stratifying by (source \u00d7 critic-refusal \u00d7 numeric-answer) barely changed it (cos 0.94), so it is not a source artifact \u2014 but it *is* 0.92-aligned with the \"critic refuses / question unanswerable\" axis.\n- Per-cell estimates revealed the important structure: the musique and refusal cells (44% of episodes) share one axis `t2` (gaps 0.5\u20131.8 sd), while the majority synthetic/non-refusal cell (56%) has a **near-orthogonal** axis `t6` (cos 0.13), on which `t2` is nearly useless (0.05 sd). Split-half checks: `t2` stable at cos 0.996, `t6` at 0.91 with honest OOS gap 0.274. Projecting onto top principal subspaces did not improve either, so I used raw estimates.\n- Target = `normalize(t2 + t6)`, equal weight: because the axes are near-orthogonal, each keeps ~0.75 projection, so this preserves `t2`'s strong separation in the small cells (1.66/0.44/1.72) *and* recovers the majority cell (0.05 \u2192 0.32). Honest reweighting checks were flat between w=1.0 and 1.5, so I kept the simple equal mix.\n\n**The edit.** Each of the 20 rows was rotated by exactly 17.3\u00b0 (cos 0.955, length preserved) toward `normalize(\u00b1behavior_axis \u2212 u_set)`: the first term builds signal, the second cancels the set's old non-behavior direction *coherently across the 10 rows* (each row carries ~0.32 of `\u015c`, and 10 rotations of 0.30 nearly erase a sum of norm 3.2). That two-term choice, with equal weight \u03b2=1, raised `cos(normalize(u_A \u2212 u_R), behavior_axis)` from **0.11 to 0.959** \u2014 96% of the theoretical maximum \u2014 whereas rotating toward the axis alone gave only 0.856.\n\n**One thing I tested and rejected.** At full strength, \"answers matching neither\" rose 14 \u2192 47, so I tried a 70%-strength variant (9.2\u00b0 rotation, remainder carried by the original, empirically near-inert direction). Derailment fell 47 \u2192 24 but flips fell 24 \u2192 14 and the margin +4.91 \u2192 +1.32: over-steering was *not* the binding constraint, the response is steeply monotone in push strength. I restored full strength and verified the host state is bit-identical to the measured configuration.\n\n## Result and expectation\n\nConfirmed on the 200 train episodes: **24 flips vs 13 unedited**, improved in both directions (adopted\u2192retains 15 vs 10; retained\u2192adopts 9 vs 3), with mean candidate-margin change **+4.91 vs \u22121.12**.\n\nI expect this to transfer, since the axis came from 25k episodes with split-half cos \u2265 0.91 and is not a source confound. The margin gain (+6 nats swing) should be the most reliable part; flip counts will be noisy. The main residual weakness is that ~24% of episodes now answer something matching neither candidate \u2014 plausibly refusals, since the axis is substantially the \"unanswerable\" axis; a cleaner refusal-stripped axis exists but loses almost all separation in the refusal cells (1.66 \u2192 0.10 sd), so I judged it a worse trade.",
